362-
363-Packet rule:
364:- one packet should solve one control problem
365:- if packet overhead exceeds its decision value, simplify or drop it
366:- if a task family has a required packet floor, do not silently downgrade it to an optional convenience note
367:- `PROMPT_guideline` direct packet floor matrix owns first-pass `required / recommended / optional packet` decisions; `Packet compliance report` audits observed-vs-required coverage, omission findings, and claim downgrades against that floor
368:- if the required packet exists but the recommended companion is missing, keep the narrower companion-missing language rather than full-strength claim language
369-- if future behavior or reusable defaults are being changed, pair `Learning-signal review memo` with `Adaptation decision memo` rather than silently mutating defaults
370:- if async or delegated work remains active, prefer lifecycle packets over prose-only status narration
371:- host-runtime maintenance and release reviews should still be able to see whether packet families cover the active control boundaries
372:- keep guide / runtime / skill lookup parity visible for goal, recovery, approval, budget, prioritization, readiness, and lifecycle-audit packets; do not let one layer silently know a family the others no longer expose
373:- keep coding-briefing, research-transparency, resource-concurrency, packet-compliance, replay-review, release-evidence, benchmark-registry, context-sufficiency, critique-quality, adaptation-promotion, and route-quality packets equally visible when those are the live control boundaries
374:- keep benchmark-execution, replay-verdict, context-failure, critique-utility, adaptation-lifecycle, route-reprioritization, coding-proof, release-v2, and telemetry-trend packets equally visible when those are the live control boundaries
375:- packet presence is not operational proof; if execution, controller behavior, or promotion evidence is the live question, choose the artifact that actually carries the linked evidence
376:- distinguish `light review memo`, `stronger packet`, and `operational artifact` rather than treating every packet as equal-strength evidence
377-- if a lighter memo and a stronger artifact address the same control problem, keep the stronger artifact active and background or supersede the lighter memo
378:- if the task has crossed from packet presence review into operational proof review, prefer the stronger operational artifact and mark weaker packets as superseded rather than silently doubling them
379-- if two compatible artifacts share one lineage, let the newer artifact supersede the stale predecessor explicitly
380:- treat `Benchmark cohort manifest`, `Replay runner verdict sheet`, `Adaptation controller audit packet`, `Coding benchmark execution ledger`, `Release promotion decision record`, `Telemetry drift investigation memo`, `Route-switch benchmark verdict`, and `Context substrate scorecard` as the direct packet floor before benchmark-grade, replay-grade, controller-grade, coding-proof-grade, release-grade, drift-grade, route-quality-grade, or retrieval-substrate-grade language
381:- keep shared identifiers such as `scenario_id`, `run_id`, `cohort_id`, `trace_id`, and `artifact_version` stable whenever benchmark, replay, adaptation, release, or telemetry packets must be linked across rounds
382-- when artifacts are joined, check precedence, compatibility, freshness, and completeness first; reject incompatible merges and preserve upstream source IDs and `artifact_version` in the surviving artifact
383-- keep failure classes such as `false-promotion`, `false-hold`, `drift-triggered review`, `rollback aftermath`, `route-switch failure`, `late clarification`, and `failed fallback timing` independently diagnosable when those control surfaces are active
